# Plan: server-name / expected-identity check (issue #9)

## Status

- Tracks issue #9 of `socket-level-wrapper-MQC/README-mqc-issues.md`.
- Wire-format unchanged.  Adds a client-side check that the
  cert returned by the server vouches for the hostname the
  client dialed.
- Independent of issues #1–#8 in implementation, but the
  property the plan asserts ("authorized for the service I
  intended to reach") is meaningful only after issue #1
  guarantees the cert isn't substitutable mid-handshake.
- No flag day required.

## What's broken today

The MQC handshake authenticates that the cert at `cert_index`
exists in the log and that the peer holds the matching ML-DSA-87
private key.  It does NOT authenticate that this cert was the one
the **caller intended** to talk to.

Concrete attack:

1. Client dials `factsorlie.com:8446` (DNS resolves to IP A).
2. Active attacker sits on the wire, redirects the connection to
   IP B.
3. IP B is itself a legitimate MQC server with a different
   `cert_index` Y, valid in the same log, holding the matching
   private key.
4. The handshake completes successfully.  Server Y signed every
   transcript byte under issue-1's binding.  Cert Y verifies via
   the inclusion proof.  Cert Y is not revoked.  No spec-defined
   check fires.
5. Client now believes it is talking to "factsorlie.com," but
   it's talking to whoever owns IP B (and cert Y).

The handshake is doing exactly what the spec says: authenticating
that the **identity in the log** matches the **bytes on the
wire**.  But "is this the identity I dialed?" is a separate
property that today nothing enforces.

## What MTC certs carry

MTC certs carry one or more **subject names** in the assertion
body — DNS names that the cert vouches for.  Spec
`mtc-keymaster/README.md` documents this; the reference
implementation has the field in `~/.TPM/<subject>/certificate.json`
under a `subject` or `assertion.dns_names` style key (exact JSON
shape: see `mtc-keymaster/server2/c/mtc_assertion.c`).

So the verifier already has the data needed to check "does this
cert claim the hostname I dialed?"  It just isn't checking.

## Target design

### Client-side flow

Client tools that initiate MQC connections (`show-tpm`,
`bootstrap_ca`, `bootstrap_leaf`, `admin_recosign`, `mqc`) all
take a `--server <host[:port]>` argument or default to
`MQC_DEFAULT_SERVER`.  The host portion is the **expected name**.

Add to `mqc_ctx_t`:

```c
struct mqc_ctx {
    /* ...existing... */
    char *expected_name;   /* hostname the caller intended to reach;
                              NULL = no check */
};
```

Add to `mqc_connect` / `mqc_connect_encrypted` — after
`mqc_peer_verify` returns the verified cert and pubkey:

```c
if (ctx->expected_name) {
    if (mqc_cert_name_matches(cert, ctx->expected_name) != 0) {
        MQC_SECURITY("CERT_NAME_MISMATCH expected=%s peer_index=%d",
                     ctx->expected_name, peer_index);
        goto fail;
    }
}
```

`mqc_cert_name_matches`:

```c
/* Returns 0 if any of the cert's DNS names matches expected
 * (case-insensitive, exact match — wildcards NOT supported in
 * v0).  Returns -1 otherwise. */
int mqc_cert_name_matches(const struct mtc_cert *cert,
                          const char *expected);
```

Wildcards (`*.example.com`) deliberately not supported in v0:

- The MTC subject schema does not currently carry wildcard
  syntax, so introducing it on the verifier side without a
  spec-defined wildcard format would be a unilateral extension.
- Wildcard semantics (label-only? IDNA?) is its own design
  rabbit hole; not justified for a single-deployment v0.
- If a cert needs to vouch for multiple hostnames, list them
  all explicitly.

### Server-side flow

No change.  The server doesn't know what name the client dialed
— it knows its own cert_index and the bytes it sent.  Server-name
binding is purely a client-side check.

(In TLS this corresponds to the client-side hostname check, not
the server-side SNI handling.)

### Handle the IP-address-vs-hostname case

Some operators dial `192.0.2.1:8446` directly.  In that case
`expected_name` is an IP literal.  MTC certs normally don't carry
IP names (they're DNS-name-based), so the match would fail.

Two options:

A. **Strict**: IP literal in `--server` → no name check is
   *possible* (cert has DNS names only); fail closed.  Operators
   who need to dial IPs MUST opt out via a separate flag
   (`--no-name-check` or `--expected-name <hostname>`).
B. **Auto-skip**: IP literal → silently skip the name check.
   Risky — operators might not realize their IP-dial path has no
   name protection.

The plan picks **A** with a clear diagnostic.  The opt-out flag
is `--expected-name <hostname>` to override the default
"derived from --server" behavior.

### CLI integration

For each MQC client tool:

- Default `expected_name` = host portion of `--server`
  (everything before the colon).  IP literals trigger fail-closed
  unless overridden.
- Optional `--expected-name <hostname>` flag explicitly sets it.
- Optional `--no-name-check` flag sets `ctx->expected_name = NULL`
  to disable the check.  Logged at handshake time as
  `MQC_SECURITY "NAME_CHECK_DISABLED"` so the dangerous mode is
  visible in `journalctl`.

### Spec edits (`draft-page-mqc-protocol-00.md`)

§10 — add new step §10.7 (Expected-Identity Check):

> ### 10.7. Expected-Identity Check (Client-Side)
>
> A client initiating an MQC connection has, by virtue of
> dialing a particular hostname, an expectation about which
> identity the peer should be.  After the cert is verified per
> §10.1-§10.6, the client MUST check that at least one of the
> cert's subject DNS names case-insensitively equals the
> hostname the client dialed.
>
> Wildcard subjects (`*.example.com`) are not defined in this
> revision; subjects MUST be exact-match.  An IP-address dial
> target has no equivalent name-check semantics; clients
> dialing an IP literal MUST either supply an explicit
> expected hostname out of band or disable the check
> explicitly.
>
> Implementations MAY skip the §10.7 check entirely (e.g., for
> peer-discovery scenarios where the dial target is known by
> `cert_index` rather than hostname) but MUST log a warning
> when doing so.
>
> Server-side endpoints have no equivalent check: the server
> does not know what the client intended to reach.  Mutual
> name-checking is a layer above MQC (e.g., the client
> includes its expected hostname in an authenticated
> application-layer claim).

§12 — extend security considerations with §12.9 (Identity vs
Authority):

> A successful MQC handshake authenticates that the peer is
> *some* legitimately-logged identity.  It does not by itself
> authenticate that the peer is the *intended* identity for the
> caller's request.  The §10.7 expected-identity check closes
> this gap on the client side.  Operators dialing by IP address
> or `cert_index` directly bypass the check and assume the
> binding-to-intent themselves.

## Code edits

### `socket-level-wrapper-MQC/mqc.h`

```c
/* Set on the ctx before mqc_connect/_encrypted; NULL = no check
 * (logged as warning). */
int  mqc_ctx_set_expected_name(mqc_ctx_t *ctx, const char *hostname);
```

### `socket-level-wrapper-MQC/mqc.c`

`mqc_ctx_t` gains the `expected_name` field; the setter is a
straight `strdup` + free-on-replace.

`mqc_connect` and `mqc_connect_encrypted` post-verify hook:

```c
if (ctx->expected_name) {
    /* mtc_cert is in scope from the verify path */
    if (mqc_cert_name_matches(&mtc_cert, ctx->expected_name) != 0) {
        MQC_SECURITY("CERT_NAME_MISMATCH expected=%s peer_index=%d",
                     ctx->expected_name, peer_index);
        goto fail;
    }
} else {
    MQC_SECURITY("NAME_CHECK_DISABLED peer_index=%d", peer_index);
}
```

### `socket-level-wrapper-MQC/mqc_peer.c`

`mqc_cert_name_matches` walks the `subject.dns_names` array
(JSON: `cert->assertion->dns_names` or whatever the live schema
is) and case-insensitively compares each entry to `expected`.

Use `strcasecmp`; clamp expected length to `MQC_MAX_HOSTNAME`
(define as 253 per RFC 1035).

### CLI tools

Each client tool's `main` parses `--server`, splits host vs port:

```c
/* host_only points into argv[]; do not free */
char *host_only = strdup(argv[server_idx]);
char *colon = strchr(host_only, ':');
if (colon) *colon = '\0';

if (!is_ip_literal(host_only)) {
    mqc_ctx_set_expected_name(ctx, host_only);
} else {
    /* IP literal: require explicit override */
    if (!opt_expected_name && !opt_no_name_check) {
        fprintf(stderr,
                "%s: --server is an IP literal; supply "
                "--expected-name <hostname> or --no-name-check\n",
                argv[0]);
        exit(2);
    }
    if (opt_expected_name) mqc_ctx_set_expected_name(ctx, opt_expected_name);
    /* opt_no_name_check leaves expected_name == NULL */
}
free(host_only);
```

`is_ip_literal` is a one-liner that calls `inet_pton` on AF_INET
and AF_INET6 and returns true if either succeeds.

## Tests

1. **happy-name-match** — server cert vouches for
   `factsorlie.com`; client dials `factsorlie.com:8446`;
   handshake succeeds.
2. **happy-name-match-case-insensitive** — same, client dials
   `Factsorlie.COM:8446`; succeeds.
3. **neg-name-mismatch** — server cert vouches for
   `factsorlie.com`; client dials `evilhost.com:8446` (DNS
   pointed at the same server); handshake aborts with
   `CERT_NAME_MISMATCH`.
4. **neg-substituted-server** — two real servers in the same
   log, certs A and B; client dials A's hostname; attacker
   redirects TCP to B's address; B's cert (correctly verified,
   correctly cosigned, correctly inclusion-proven) doesn't
   list A's hostname; client aborts at §10.7.  This is the
   substituted-server attack the plan exists to defeat.
5. **happy-explicit-expected-name** — client dials by IP +
   `--expected-name factsorlie.com`; succeeds if cert lists
   that name.
6. **neg-ip-no-override** — client dials by IP, no
   `--expected-name`, no `--no-name-check`; tool exits with
   error before opening the connection.
7. **happy-no-name-check-explicit** — client passes
   `--no-name-check`; handshake succeeds with a `NAME_CHECK_DISABLED`
   warning in journalctl.
8. **neg-wildcard-not-honored** — cert lists `*.example.com`;
   client dials `foo.example.com`; check fails (wildcards
   explicitly unsupported in v0).

## Migration runbook

Per the deployment-model rule, this is wire-quiet *and*
behavior-conservative for the typical case (clients always have
a hostname in `--server`; the cert presumably already lists that
hostname).

1. Build + install: `./make-all.sh`.
2. `sudo systemctl restart mtc-ca.service` (server side: no
   behavior change, pure recompile).
3. Smoke test from the dev box: `show-tpm --verify` against
   `factsorlie.com:8446`.  Should succeed without warnings.
4. Negative test: temporarily edit `/etc/hosts` to point
   `evilhost.com` at factsorlie.com's IP; `show-tpm --server
   evilhost.com:8446 --verify`.  Should fail with
   `CERT_NAME_MISMATCH`.
5. If any deployed peer's cert does NOT list its dial hostname,
   the upgrade will break that peer's clients.  Fix by
   regenerating the cert with the missing name (operator task,
   one-time per affected cert).  For factsorlie.com specifically,
   verify before deploy that all logged certs list their
   public hostname.

## Verification

1. **Zero-warning build**.
2. **`make check`** clean.
3. **All issue-9 tests pass** (4 happy + 4 negative).
4. **Service health**: `systemctl is-active mtc-ca.service` →
   `active`.
5. **End-to-end**: `show-tpm --verify` against
   factsorlie.com:8446 succeeds.
6. **Pre-deploy audit**: confirm every deployed peer's cert
   lists its public hostname.  Run via `show-tpm --list-peers`
   or equivalent introspection.

## Critical files to modify

1. `socket-level-wrapper-MQC/mqc.c` — `mqc_ctx_t` field,
   setter, two post-verify hooks (clear and encrypted).
2. `socket-level-wrapper-MQC/mqc_peer.c` —
   `mqc_cert_name_matches`.
3. `socket-level-wrapper-MQC/mqc.h` — setter prototype, hostname
   length constant.
4. Each MQC client tool (`mtc-keymaster/tools/c/show-tpm.c`,
   `bootstrap_ca.c`, `bootstrap_leaf.c`, `admin_recosign.c`,
   `mtc-keymaster/tools/c/mqc.c` if separate) — `--server`
   parse, `--expected-name`, `--no-name-check` flags.
5. `socket-level-wrapper-MQC/draft-page-mqc-protocol-00.md` —
   new §10.7, §12.9.

## Out of scope (deliberately deferred)

- **Wildcard subjects** — would require a wildcard-syntax spec
  and a tested matcher.  Not justified for v0.
- **IDNA / Unicode hostnames** — MTC subject schema does not
  currently define IDNA semantics.  ASCII hostnames only in v0.
- **SAN-style alternative-name extensions** — the MTC subject
  schema is simpler than X.509; extending it is its own change.
- **Mutual name-check** (server-side validation that the client
  was reached at the expected hostname) — server doesn't have
  the data to do this; would require an authenticated
  application-layer claim from the client.
- **Connection-pinning by cert_index** — would let a client
  bypass §10.7 by remembering "I trust cert_index Y for this
  hostname"; useful but a separate feature, not a v0 default.
